Keep the text off the screen's edge, and sit letters at the face's advance.

kmscon fills the screen with as many cells as fit, so text runs to its
edge. Two options, --padding-x and --padding-y, keep that many pixels clear
beside the text and above and below it: the columns and rows are counted
in what's left, and the renderers already centre the cells, so the clear
space comes out even on both sides. A padding that would leave no room for
a single cell is dropped rather than leaving the screen empty.

Pango's cell was the face's average advance, rounded down, and a pixel
more, so a monospace face sat a pixel looser than in other terminals. It is
now the advance rounded up, which is what they draw.

